<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amílcar Cabral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Amilcar Cabral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amílcar Cabral was an agronomist, political thinker and anti-colonial leader from Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paigc">PAIGC</a> in 1956 and from 1963 led its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against Portuguese rule, combining military struggle with schools, clinics and people's courts in liberated areas. He was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a> in January 1973, months before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a> declared independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amilcar-cabral.jpg" alt="Amílcar Cabral" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cabral_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
